# Target C — endpoint route-excess and near-equilateral teeth audit

**Date:** 2026-09-12
**LEVEL I STATUS:** **STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.**

This module independently audits and repairs the proof-only endpoint route-excess
report that logically precedes the corridor bridge/rhombus split.  The scalar and
boundary-arclength argument is valid.  One sentence in the original report was too
strong: a two-edge outer-face route between consecutive loaded attachments need not
have an unloaded middle vertex when cut vertices are present.  The error is harmless
at the Section-96 endpoint because any loaded middle vertex uses loaded-loaded contact
edges, and canonical Section 96 gives only `o(D)` such edges.

The corrected theorem is therefore:

> At the sharp Section-96 wrapping endpoint, the attachment-polygon perimeter is
> `3 alpha_4 D+o(D)`, the outer-walk excess is `alpha_4 D+o(D)`, and
> `(alpha_4-o(1))D` ordinary boundary intervals are two-edge unit-contact teeth with
> an **unloaded** apex and base length `1+o(1)` but strictly greater than one.

Here

\[
\theta_4=2\arcsin\mu_4,
\qquad
\mu_4=0.936215741409583\ldots,
\qquad
\alpha_4=3\theta_4-2\pi={3\over2}\sin\theta_4.
\]

---

# 97. Six-cluster saturation localizes the endpoint outer-walk excess

## 97.1 Canonical endpoint data

Let `P_D=conv(A_D)` be a wrapping Type-II harmonic cell with

\[
{\operatorname{diam}(P_D)\over D}\to\mu_4.
\]

Write `a_D=|A_D|`, let `L_D` be the outer-face walk length of the induced harmonic
contact graph, and retain the canonical local currencies `s_D,c_D,Q_D` and
`c_D^partial<=c_D`.

Canonical Section 96 gives

\[
{a_D\over D}\to\alpha_4,
\qquad
{s_D\over D},{c_D\over D},{Q_D\over D}\to0,
\qquad
{L_D\over D}\to4\alpha_4.
\tag{97.1}
\]

It also gives three distinguished forbidden attachment gaps.  Put

\[
\phi_4:=2\pi-2\theta_4.
\tag{97.2}
\]

The three corresponding outer-contact routes and endpoint chords each have length

\[
D\sin\theta_4+o(D).
\tag{97.3}
\]

## 97.2 Equality in the six-cluster count saturates the allowed boundary arcs

Use the three wrapping anchors from canonical Sections 88 and 94.  Their six allowed
circular intervals have total angular measure

\[
6\theta_4-4\pi=2\alpha_4.
\tag{97.4}
\]

All finite attachment vertices lie in an `o(1)` angular enlargement of those six
intervals.  Type-II annular convergence and the Section-88 polar-arc bound therefore
place them in at most six force-hull boundary arcs of total arclength `S_D` satisfying

\[
S_D\le\alpha_4D+o(D).
\tag{97.5}
\]

Merge touching allowed intervals into one component.  If one component contains `N`
attachment vertices, the boundary arclength from its first to its last attachment is
at least `N-1`: each consecutive source pair has Euclidean distance at least one and
boundary arclength dominates chord length.  There are at most six components, so

\[
S_D\ge a_D-6=\alpha_4D+o(D).
\tag{97.6}
\]

Thus

\[
\boxed{S_D=\alpha_4D+o(D).}
\tag{97.7}
\]

The difference between the two sides is a sum of nonnegative spacing excesses and
unused endpoint margins, up to an `O(1)` component-count correction.  Hence their
total is `o(D)`.  Because the force-hull boundary stays at radius
`(1/2+o(1))D`, each of the finitely many endpoint angular margins is `o(1)`.
Consequently the three finite forbidden gaps satisfy

\[
\boxed{h_{i,D}=\phi_4+o(1),\qquad i=1,2,3.}
\tag{97.8}

## 97.3 Exact attachment-polygon perimeter at the endpoint

The three polygon sides crossing the forbidden gaps have total length

\[
3D\sin\theta_4+o(D)=2\alpha_4D+o(D).
\tag{97.9}
\]

Every other polygon side joins consecutive attachments inside one allowed boundary
component, so its chord is no longer than the corresponding force-hull boundary arc.
Therefore the sum `P_D^short` of the remaining `a_D-3` sides satisfies

\[
P_D^{short}\le S_D+o(D)=\alpha_4D+o(D).
\tag{97.10}
\]

Unit separation gives the reverse bound

\[
P_D^{short}\ge a_D-3=\alpha_4D+o(D).
\tag{97.11}
\]

Hence

\[
\boxed{
P_D^{short}=\alpha_4D+o(D),
\qquad
{\operatorname{per}(P_D)\over D}\to3\alpha_4.
}
\tag{97.12}

The Section-96 endpoint therefore does **not** satisfy
`per(P_D)/D ->4alpha_4`; the local perimeter capacity retains a linear gap.
Together with (97.1),

\[
\boxed{
{L_D-\operatorname{per}(P_D)\over D}\to\alpha_4.
}
\tag{97.13}

## 97.4 Almost every ordinary interval is a two-edge route

The three special routes have total path length

\[
3D\sin\theta_4+o(D)=2\alpha_4D+o(D).
\tag{97.14}
\]

Among the remaining ordinary attachment intervals, only `c_D^partial=o(D)` can be
one-edge direct loaded-loaded contacts.  Every other interval has route length at
least two.  Since `L_D=4alpha_4D+o(D)` and there are
`a_D-3=alpha_4D+o(D)` ordinary intervals, the total excess above this minimum route
budget is `o(D)`.  Route lengths are integers, so

\[
\boxed{
\#\{\text{ordinary routes of length at least three}\}=o(D).
}
\tag{97.15}

Thus `alpha_4D-o(D)` ordinary intervals have outer route exactly

\[
p_k-y_k-p_{k+1}
\tag{97.16}
\]

with both contact edges of length one.

## 97.5 Repair: discard the `o(D)` routes with a loaded middle vertex

The original report asserted directly that every middle vertex `y_k` in (97.16) is
unloaded.  Section 62 alone does not justify that in a graph with cut vertices: an
outer-face route between consecutive convex-hull attachments can revisit another
loaded attachment.

The endpoint ledger repairs the issue.  If `y_k` is loaded, then `y_k in A_D` and both
edges `p_ky_k` and `y_kp_{k+1}` are loaded-loaded contacts counted by `c_D`.  A
loaded-loaded contact edge has at most two outer-face occurrences, so the number of
length-two route occurrences with a loaded middle vertex is at most `2c_D=o(D)`.

Discard those exceptional routes.  We retain

\[
\boxed{(\alpha_4-o(1))D}
\tag{97.17}

length-two ordinary routes whose middle vertex `x_k` is unloaded.

## 97.6 The retained routes are near-equilateral unit-unit teeth

Let

\[
d_{k,D}:=|p_k-p_{k+1}|
\]

be the attachment-polygon side under an ordinary route.  From (97.12),

\[
\sum_{k\ ordinary}(d_{k,D}-1)=o(D),
\qquad d_{k,D}\ge1.
\tag{97.18}
\]

Therefore there is a sequence `epsilon_D->0` such that, after discarding another
`o(D)` ordinary intervals,

\[
1\le d_{k,D}\le1+\epsilon_D.
\tag{97.19}
\]

Intersect with the unloaded length-two routes from §97.5.  For
`(alpha_4-o(1))D` intervals,

\[
|p_k-x_k|=|x_k-p_{k+1}|=1,
\qquad
1<|p_k-p_{k+1}|\le1+\epsilon_D.
\tag{97.20}
\]

The strict lower inequality follows from triangle-freeness: equality would make
`p_k,x_k,p_{k+1}` a unit equilateral contact triangle.

The apex angle and altitude are therefore

\[
\angle p_kx_kp_{k+1}
=2\arcsin(d_{k,D}/2)
=\pi/3+o(1),
\tag{97.21}
\]

\[
\operatorname{dist}(x_k,[p_k,p_{k+1}])
=\sqrt{1-d_{k,D}^2/4}
=\sqrt3/2+o(1).
\tag{97.22}
\]

Hence the sharp Section-96 endpoint has the first-order boundary form

```text
three macroscopic near-geodesic contact corridors
+
(alpha_4-o(1)) D near-equilateral unit-unit teeth with unloaded apices.
```

## 97.7 Audit conclusion

The theorem uses only canonical Sections 62, 88, 94, and 96 plus Type-II annular
convergence and unit separation.  It does not use a KKT coefficient floor, the
rejected Reuleaux-cap route, or any claim that the whole outer walk is geodesic.

The sole defect found in the earlier self-audit was the loaded-middle-vertex sentence;
§97.5 repairs it without changing the asymptotic theorem.

**Status:** independently audited proof-only theorem; lifecycle state unchanged.
